{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-001","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:2|1:6","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"temel","metin":"Mezmur 23, Tanrı'yı {he:יְהוָ֥ה רֹ֝עִ֗י, tr:Adonay ro'i, gloss:Rab çobanımdır, source:WLC:Ps.23.1} diye anar; ardından yeşil otlakta yatırmayı ve doğru izlerde yürütmeyi anlatır. Şerhte ayrı köklerden çıkan sahip, otlak ve yol gösterici görüntüleri bu mezmurda tek bir çobanlık sahnesine dönüşür.","kaynak":"WLC:Ps.23.1|WLC:Ps.23.2|WLC:Ps.23.3","paragraf":51,"capa":"Sürü bağlı olduğu yerde kalır","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-002","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:2|1:7","islev":"karsit","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"Eyüp 39'da yabani eşeği bağlarından kimin saldığı sorulur; kır onun evidir, dağlarda ot arar. Aynı konuşma yaban öküzünün insanın yemliğinde kalıp ona hizmet etmeyeceğini söyler. {he:מִֽי־שִׁלַּ֣ח פֶּ֣רֶא חָפְשִׁ֑י, tr:mi şillaḥ pere ḥofşi, gloss:yabani eşeği kim özgür bıraktı, source:WLC:Job.39.5} sorusu, evcil ve yaban sürülerinin ayrımını insan sahipliğinin sınırı olarak gösterir.","kaynak":"WLC:Job.39.5|WLC:Job.39.6|WLC:Job.39.8|WLC:Job.39.9|WLC:Job.39.10","paragraf":51,"capa":"Sahipli sürünün dışında","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-INC-MTF-001","gelenek":"incil","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:2|1:6","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"temel","metin":"Yuhanna 10'da çoban kendi koyunlarını adlarıyla çağırıp dışarı çıkarır; önlerinde yürür, onlar sesini tanıyıp izler, yabancıyı izlemez. {el:ἔμπροσθεν αὐτῶν πορεύεται, καὶ τὰ πρόβατα αὐτῷ ἀκολουθεῖ, tr:emprosthen autôn poreuetai kai ta probata autô akolouthei, gloss:önlerinde yürür ve koyunlar onu izler, source:SBLGNT:John.10.4} sözü, şerhteki öncü ve ona bağlı sürü hareketinin İncil'deki insan-çoban karşılığıdır.","kaynak":"SBLGNT:John.10.3|SBLGNT:John.10.4|SBLGNT:John.10.5","paragraf":51,"capa":"Sürüde bir öncü yürür","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"yunanca_ahit"}
{"id":"S001-INC-MTF-002","gelenek":"incil","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:2|1:6","islev":"aciklama","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"Elçilerin İşleri 20:28, Tanrı'nın sürüsünü edinmiş olduğunu söylerken gözeticileri onu gütmekle görevlendirir; I. Petrus 5:2-3 de bu görevi tahakküm yerine örnek olmak diye sınırlar. {el:ποιμάνατε τὸ ἐν ὑμῖν ποίμνιον τοῦ θεοῦ, tr:poimanate to en hymin poimnion tou theou, gloss:aranızdaki Tanrı sürüsünü güdün, source:SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2} buyruğu, sürünün sahibiyle ona önderlik edenin ayrılabildiğini gösterir.","kaynak":"SBLGNT:Acts.20.28|SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2|SBLGNT:1Pet.5.3","paragraf":51,"capa":"Bir deve sürüsü bir sahibe aittir","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"yunanca_ahit"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-PRL-001","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"paralel","ayet":"1:7","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"temel","metin":"Tesniye 22:1-2, yolunu yitirmiş öküz ya da koyunun görmezden gelinmeyip sahibine döndürülmesini buyurur. Sahibi yakında değilse veya tanınmıyorsa bulan kişi hayvanı evinde tutar. {he:וְלֹ֣א יְדַעְתּ֑וֹ וַאֲסַפְתּוֹ֙ אֶל־תּ֣וֹךְ בֵּיתֶ֔ךָ, tr:ve-lo yeda'to va-asafto el tokh beitekha, gloss:onu tanımıyorsan evine al, source:WLC:Deut.22.2} sözü, şerhte sahibinin kim olduğu bilinmeyen devenin hukukî karşı sahnesidir.","kaynak":"WLC:Deut.22.1|WLC:Deut.22.2","paragraf":52,"capa":"Sonra bir deve kopar gider","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-003","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:7","islev":"sonuc","iliski":"tematik","durum":"yorum","kat":"temel","metin":"Mezmur 119:176'nın konuşanı {he:תָּעִ֗יתִי כְּשֶׂ֣ה אֹ֭בֵד בַּקֵּ֣שׁ עַבְדֶּ֑ךָ, tr:ta'iti ke-se oved baqqeş avdekha, gloss:kaybolmuş koyun gibi saptım; kulunu ara, source:WLC:Ps.119.176} diye yakarır. Hezekiel 34:16'da Tanrı kaybolanı arayıp dağılmışı döndüreceğini bildirir. Şerhteki sahibi belirsiz hayvan görüntüsü, burada sahibine yöneltilen arama duasına ve onun cevabına dönüşür.","kaynak":"WLC:Ps.119.176|WLC:Ezek.34.16","paragraf":52,"capa":"Başıboş kalan deve şöyle tanımlanır","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-INC-PRL-001","gelenek":"incil","tur":"paralel","ayet":"1:7","islev":"sonuc","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"temel","metin":"Luka 15:4-7'de bir adam yüz koyunundan kaybolan birini bulana dek arar, bulunca omzuna alır ve sevinir; anlatı bu dönüşü tövbe eden insanla ilişkilendirir. {el:πορεύεται ἐπὶ τὸ ἀπολωλὸς ἕως εὕρῃ αὐτό, tr:poreuetai epi to apolôlos heôs heurê auto, gloss:kaybolanın peşinden bulana dek gider, source:SBLGNT:Luke.15.4} ifadesi, şerhin kayıp hayvan sahnesine arayan sahibin eylemini ekler.","kaynak":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.4|SBLGNT:Luke.15.5|SBLGNT:Luke.15.6|SBLGNT:Luke.15.7","paragraf":52,"capa":"Sonra bir deve kopar gider","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"yunanca_ahit"}
{"id":"S001-INC-MTF-003","gelenek":"incil","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:2|1:7","islev":"aciklama","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"Yuhanna 10:12-14'te koyunların kendisine ait olmadığı ücretli bakıcı kurt gelince kaçar; iyi çoban ise kendi koyunlarını tanır ve onlar da onu tanır. {el:οὗ οὐκ ⸀ἔστιν τὰ πρόβατα ἴδια, tr:hou ouk estin ta probata idia, gloss:koyunlar kendisinin değildir, source:SBLGNT:John.10.12} ayrımı, sahibinin bilinmemesiyle sahipsiz bırakılmanın aynı şey olmadığını düşündürür.","kaynak":"SBLGNT:John.10.12|SBLGNT:John.10.14","paragraf":52,"capa":"Surenin sahibe verdiği iki ad","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"yunanca_ahit"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-004","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:6","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"temel","metin":"Sayılar 27:17'de Musa, topluluğun önünde yürüyüp onları götürecek bir önder ister; gerekçesi, halkın çobansız koyunlar gibi kalmamasıdır. {he:אֲשֶׁר־יֵצֵ֣א לִפְנֵיהֶ֗ם, tr:aşer yetse lifneihem, gloss:onların önünde yürüyen, source:WLC:Num.27.17} sözü, şerhteki hâdînin önden gitmesi ile insan topluluğunun yol gösterici ihtiyacını aynı resimde buluşturur.","kaynak":"WLC:Num.27.17","paragraf":53,"capa":"Sürü, önde yürüyen hâdînin","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-005","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:6|1:7","islev":"karsit","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"Yeremya 50:6'da halk kayıp koyunlara benzetilir; onları saptıran kendi çobanlarıdır ve koyunlar yatacakları yeri unutmuştur. {he:רֹעֵיהֶ֣ם הִתְע֔וּם, tr:ro'eihem hit'um, gloss:çobanları onları saptırdı, source:WLC:Jer.50.6} sözü, önden gidenin varlığının tek başına doğru yönü garanti etmediğini gösterir. Burada WLC'nin yazılı metni esas alınır; varyantlar ayrıdır.","kaynak":"WLC:Jer.50.6","paragraf":53,"capa":"ardından gidilecek bir öncüyü de ister","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-006","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:6|1:7","islev":"destek","iliski":"lafzi","durum":"acik","kat":"temel","metin":"Mezmur 27:11'deki dua {he:וּ֭נְחֵנִי בְּאֹ֣רַח מִישׁ֑וֹר, tr:unḥeni be-oraḥ mişor, gloss:beni düz yolda ilet, source:WLC:Ps.27.11} biçimindedir. Mezmur 107:4-7'de ise çölde yolunu yitirenler Rabbe seslenir ve O onları düz yoldan yerleşecekleri kente yürütür. Şerhin ıssız yer, isteme ve iletilme görüntüsü bu iki mezmurda dua ve kurtuluş anlatısı olarak belirir.","kaynak":"WLC:Ps.27.11|WLC:Ps.107.4|WLC:Ps.107.6|WLC:Ps.107.7","paragraf":53,"capa":"Altıncı ayetteki istek de bu sahnede","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-INC-MTF-004","gelenek":"incil","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:6|1:7","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"Matta 9:36'da İsa, kalabalığı {el:πρόβατα μὴ ἔχοντα ποιμένα, tr:probata mê ekhonta poimena, gloss:çobanı olmayan koyunlar, source:SBLGNT:Matt.9.36} gibi görür. Markos 6:34 aynı görüntüden sonra onlara öğretmeye başladığını belirtir. Yol göstericinin yokluğu böylece yalnız sürünün dağılması değil, öğretim ihtiyacı olarak da çizilir.","kaynak":"SBLGNT:Matt.9.36|SBLGNT:Mark.6.34","paragraf":53,"capa":"ardından gidilecek bir öncüyü de ister","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"yunanca_ahit"}
{"id":"S001-INC-MTF-005","gelenek":"incil","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:6","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"Vahiy 7:17'de Kuzu'nun kendisi çoban olur ve insanları yaşam suyu kaynaklarına götürür: {el:τὸ ἀρνίον ... ⸀ποιμανεῖ αὐτούς, καὶ ⸀ὁδηγήσει αὐτούς, tr:to arnion poimanei autous kai hodêgêsei autous, gloss:Kuzu onları güdecek ve yol gösterecek, source:SBLGNT:Rev.7.17}. Önceden yönlendirilen hayvanın yön gösterene dönüşmesi, şerhteki sürü öncüsü imgesine ayrı bir İncil biçimi verir.","kaynak":"SBLGNT:Rev.7.17","paragraf":53,"capa":"Sürü, önde yürüyen hâdînin","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"yunanca_ahit"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-007","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:2|1:7","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"temel","metin":"İşaya 1:3'te öküz sahibini, eşek efendisinin yemliğini tanır; İsrail ise tanımaz. {he:יָדַ֥ע שׁוֹר֙ קֹנֵ֔הוּ, tr:yada şor qonehu, gloss:öküz sahibini tanır, source:WLC:Isa.1.3} sözü, şerhin sonunda hayvanın sahibini tanıması ile insanın ondan daha şaşkın kalması arasındaki ters karşılaştırmaya yakın bir Tevrat örneğidir.","kaynak":"WLC:Isa.1.3","paragraf":54,"capa":"Hayvan başıboş kalsa da sürüsünü arar","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-TEV-MTF-008","gelenek":"tevrat","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:2|1:3","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"Yunus 4:11'de Tanrı Ninova'ya acımasını gerekçelendirirken şehirdeki çok sayıdaki hayvanı da anar: {he:וּבְהֵמָ֖ה רַבָּֽה, tr:uvehema rabba, gloss:ve birçok hayvan, source:WLC:Jonah.4.11}. Şerhte yük taşıyan hayvanların ardından Rabbin şefkatinin anılmasıyla bu ifade aynı eylemi anlatmaz; fakat merhametin hayvanları da kapsadığını açıkça gösterir.","kaynak":"WLC:Jonah.4.11","paragraf":54,"capa":"Hayvanlar yükü uzak bir yere taşır","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"masoretik"}
{"id":"S001-INC-MTF-006","gelenek":"incil","tur":"motif","ayet":"1:6|1:7","islev":"destek","iliski":"tematik","durum":"acik","kat":"ek","metin":"II. Petrus 2:15, {el:καταλιπόντες εὐθεῖαν ὁδὸν ἐπλανήθησαν, tr:katalipontes eutheian hodon eplanêthêsan, gloss:düz yolu bırakıp saptılar, source:SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15} der. Elçilerin İşleri 13:10'da Rabbin düz yollarını eğriltmekten söz edilir. İki metin, şerhte hayvanlardan yola geçen anlatının düz yol ve sapma karşıtlığını insan davranışı için kullanır.","kaynak":"SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15|SBLGNT:Acts.13.10","paragraf":54,"capa":"Hemen ardından yol konusu açılır","bag":"benzerlik","tarihleme":"kuran_oncesi","nusha":"yunanca_ahit"}

{"connection_id": "BC-ff6221785ca493cc2a85", "ref": "Basil of Caesarea, Hexaemeron 8:4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bees ordered under a king", "note": "Basil’s bee discussion presents the hive as an ordered community under a king; it supplies a Christian natural-theological analogue to the commentary’s malik of the bees, though section divisions vary by edition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-490832fc526f28db823d", "ref": "Gospel of Thomas 107", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "A shepherd searches for his one lost sheep", "note": "The commentary's dālle camel escapes and remains away from its owner; Thomas preserves a lost-sheep parable in which the shepherd leaves the ninety-nine and seeks the stray."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0ea5fe48fe773c35e6e", "ref": "Gospel of Thomas 107", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "one strayed sheep valued above the ninety-nine", "note": "This lost-sheep version has the shepherd seek the one that strayed and value it above the ninety-nine, a distinct Christian apocryphal intensification of the individual animal’s recovery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-381569836f9f4eecf89e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.7", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "flock-tender receives milk from the flock", "note": "Paul’s labor argument asks who tends a flock without drinking its milk. Alongside his ox citation, this turns the material benefit of a tended flock into an analogy for the guide’s rightful support."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ed83e407478115a0ac3", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.9", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "unmuzzled ox re-read for human workers", "note": "Paul invokes the rule against muzzling a grain-treading ox in an argument about the support of human workers; this Christian reading turns a working animal’s claim into a claim about labor, relevant to the commentary’s tamed, load-bearing animal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-65391ce914f79e27ac51", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Straying sheep return to the shepherd and overseer", "note": "The commentary calls the camel dālle when lost from its owner; Peter says his readers were straying like sheep but have returned to the shepherd who watches them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50a5c0222fffa6893c28", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "straying sheep return to Shepherd and Overseer", "note": "The addressees were sheep going astray but have returned to a Shepherd and Overseer; it connects wandering from the way with restoration to a guiding, responsible figure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0462890dbfe25a5d38ef", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Elders must shepherd and oversee God's flock", "note": "The commentary develops a leading animal whose herd follows it; Peter assigns human leaders a shepherd's oversight of God's flock, making the guide's duty explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e675cb933f386f1afc6", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherd the flock of God under oversight", "note": "The elders are told to shepherd the flock of God and exercise oversight, a specific Christian instruction about the responsibility attached to leading another’s flock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f61629509553ed00614f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Christ is the chief shepherd", "note": "The commentary uses malik for the animal at the head of the herd; Peter calls Christ the chief shepherd who appears above the delegated flock leaders."}
{"connection_id": "BC-83e5c5b3c079544b4fb0", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forsaking the straight way and going astray", "note": "The text says its opponents abandoned the straight way and went astray, retaining both sides of the commentary’s road vocabulary rather than merely a general moral failure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c90d00877b2fddab77cf", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.19", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seal says the Lord knows those who are his", "note": "God’s firm foundation bears a seal declaring that the Lord knows those who are his, a textual counterpart to the commentary’s visible mark and the lost camel whose owner cannot be known."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1657b149a1d54b02fe9d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.13.10", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perverting the straight paths of the Lord", "note": "The rebuke names the straight paths of the Lord and accuses its target of making them crooked, a concrete New Testament counterpart to the commentary’s path that turns aside from the qasd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89302769a55108496c84", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.28", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "overseers shepherd God’s acquired flock", "note": "The elders must shepherd God’s flock, which God acquired, while serving as overseers. It distinguishes the true owner from delegated guides in a way that closely illuminates the commentary’s rab/mālik and leading-animal relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-292503a0ca11f5168b4e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.3", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A bit directs a horse's whole body", "note": "The commentary says the horse's hādī may be its neck or the foremost group; James supplies a different concrete steering image, the bit by which a human turns a horse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f8416053e12e187eb40", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.38", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic Rabbi as an authority title", "note": "The source labels rabb as an owner-title; John retains the Semitic title Rabbi and glosses it as Teacher. The likely cognate relation supplies only a title comparison here, not evidence that John’s teacher image is a flock-owner image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-641275afd6fe413eab82", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.11", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The good shepherd gives his life for the sheep", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the known owner from the camel left without one; Jesus defines the true shepherd by risking his life for the flock that belongs to him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-970be72532fb9733c056", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.12", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A hired worker abandons sheep when a wolf comes", "note": "The commentary ties the camel to its owner and herd; John's contrast distinguishes a genuine keeper from a hired hand who does not own the sheep and flees danger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cdaca278560bcc1b3059", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.13", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The hired hand flees because the sheep are not his", "note": "The commentary's mark identifies whose camel it is; John makes ownership the reason the hired worker fails to care for the flock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e9375fc184dbe125fe9", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "owner and flock know one another", "note": "The reciprocal knowledge of shepherd and sheep gives a direct positive counterpart to the dālla whose rab and mālik cannot be identified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d909ed8138d3ff8133f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Other sheep are brought into one flock under one shepherd", "note": "The commentary contrasts owned herds with wild flocks outside a known owner; Jesus speaks of gathering other sheep so that they become one flock with one shepherd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4f3a17db8cbbf4c832f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.27", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The shepherd knows his sheep and they follow him", "note": "The commentary joins owner, animal, and following leader; Jesus says he knows his sheep and they follow his voice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0ba43fbc0e63cbfdba6", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.3", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The shepherd calls his own sheep by name", "note": "The commentary says a brand identifies whose camel it is and that the stray camel's owner is unknown; John's shepherd knows and summons each animal as his own."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1b989e81e149c39a603c", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.3", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "own sheep called by name and led out", "note": "The shepherd calls his own sheep by name and leads them out, joining recognized belonging with a leader who initiates the flock’s movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21a0ba97edf7b0be58b0", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The shepherd goes ahead and the sheep follow", "note": "The commentary's malik is the animal at the head of the herd and its hadī leads the others; Jesus describes the shepherd walking ahead and his sheep following."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c7d9425296d2b97357a", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "shepherd goes ahead and sheep follow", "note": "The shepherd goes in front and the sheep follow his voice, the closest New Testament rendering of the commentary’s front animal/hādī followed by the rest of the herd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fdafb13389f46e9b47ad", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.5", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sheep do not follow a stranger", "note": "The commentary says the herd follows its foreleader; John adds recognition as the criterion, since sheep flee from an unfamiliar voice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ff5418e17ed437c386e", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.20.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Mary addresses Jesus as ῥαββουνί", "note": "The commentary's dictionary form rabb (رَبّ) is glossed 'owner'; John's Greek preserves the Aramaic rbb title 'my teacher,' a related Semitic master-title used for a teacher rather than a livestock owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d334df44b5ffaec5240d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.21.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Peter is charged to shepherd Jesus' sheep", "note": "The commentary's herd follows a foreleader; the risen Jesus delegates care of his own sheep to Peter, distinguishing the flock's owner from its appointed shepherd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c17bd2d7549425915c39", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.13.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "owner unties ox or donkey and leads it to water", "note": "Jesus describes each person untying an ox or donkey from the stall and leading it to water, joining a known owner, a restrained domestic animal, purposeful leading, and animal provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e4f4b38984815dd160b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "A shepherd searches for one sheep that strayed", "note": "The commentary defines a dālle camel as escaped and ownerless; Jesus' parable gives the lost animal an owner who searches until he finds it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c539424ecb934bc2f6a2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "one lost sheep sought by its owner", "note": "The man who loses one of a hundred sheep goes after the lost one, a direct recovery scene for the commentary’s animal separated from its owner and herd."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f867c92be193a620b4c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.5", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "found sheep carried on shoulders", "note": "After finding the lost sheep, the shepherd lays it on his shoulders with joy; this supplies the bodily return of the escaped animal that the preceding search scene alone does not state."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8fd597ba7d5908882340", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.6", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "The found sheep is brought back and celebrated", "note": "The commentary leaves the lost camel separated from its owner; Luke's shepherd returns with the found sheep and gathers friends to celebrate, supplying a recovery scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-174bb42d1a531fce9d05", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.7", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Heaven rejoices over a sinner who repents", "note": "The commentary compares human misguidance with a camel that is lost from its owner; Luke interprets the recovered sheep as the sinner who turns back, connecting return to repentance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cc9179a811be0fc3f15", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.14.27", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jesus applies the struck-shepherd saying to his disciples' flight", "note": "The commentary says followers depend on their herd's foreleader; Mark quotes Zechariah's struck shepherd and interprets the scattered sheep as the disciples who will fall away at Jesus' arrest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7c7deed0793749f5865", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.6.34", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A shepherdless crowd receives teaching", "note": "The commentary makes a leading animal the image behind the request to be guided; Jesus sees the crowd as sheep without a shepherd and begins teaching them, acting as guide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-112631c4dd5817da6ad7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.29", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "take my yoke and learn from me", "note": "Jesus’ invitation to take his yoke and learn makes animal harness a figure for guided discipleship, offering a Christian transformation of the commentary’s subdued, neck-directed animal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa9ba980e0e84f2bec37", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.12.11", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "a sheep fallen into a pit is lifted out", "note": "Jesus assumes that an owner will take hold of and lift out a sheep fallen into a pit even on the Sabbath, a concrete recovery scene for the commentary’s animal left in an exposed place."}
{"connection_id": "BC-29c54ae757271bbad405", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.12", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "A shepherd seeks a wandering sheep", "note": "The commentary's lost camel has no known owner; Matthew's parable has a shepherd leave the ninety-nine to seek the one that wandered."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d640203e305c758c3b8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.13", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The shepherd rejoices when the sheep is found", "note": "The commentary describes the camel only while lost; Matthew emphasizes the owner's joy at recovery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a274d4dfe2b7c65bfa28", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Father wills that none of the little ones perish", "note": "The commentary contrasts a lost animal with humans more lost than cattle; Matthew uses the shepherd image to say the Father seeks the vulnerable rather than letting one perish."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7119a54676f152b21698", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.32", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The king separates people as a shepherd separates sheep and goats", "note": "The commentary places the herd-leader image beside Malik, owner of Judgment Day; Jesus' judgment scene joins royal authority with a shepherd sorting livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a06d9f5313b92f9a92f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.33", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sheep stand at the king's right and goats at his left", "note": "The commentary presents a herd under a leader and names the owner of Judgment Day; Matthew makes the judgment division visible by assigning the sheep and goats opposite sides."}

arguments:
text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Add File: /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s001/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.session-20261005/sec10/gaps.json
+{"missing_sources":["Babylonian Talmud, Bava Metzia 27a — absent from the available local snapshot; text and legal wording unverified","Basil of Caesarea, Hexaemeron 8:4 — absent from the available local snapshot; bee passage unverified","Bava Metzia 27b — absent from the available local snapshot; identifying-sign discussion unverified","Exodus Rabbah 2:2 — absent from the available local snapshot; runaway-lamb episode unverified","Gospel of Thomas 107 — absent from the available local snapshot; both proposed lost-sheep claims unverified","Mishnah Bava Metzia 2:9 — absent from the available local snapshot; legal criterion unverified","Psalms of Solomon 17:40 — absent from the available local snapshot; shepherd-ruler passage unverified","Psalms of Solomon 17:41 — absent from the available local snapshot; leading claim unverified","Sirach 18:13 — absent from the available local snapshot; both proposed shepherd-mercy claims unverified","Wisdom of Solomon 10:10 — absent from the available local snapshot; straight-path claim unverified","Wisdom of Solomon 11:26 — absent from the available local snapshot; universal-ownership claim unverified"],"not_found":[],"unresolved":[]}
*** End Patch"));

text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Add File: /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s001/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.session-20261005/sec10/verdicts.jsonl
+{"connection_id":"BC-ff6221785ca493cc2a85","ref":"Basil of Caesarea, Hexaemeron 8:4","status":"unavailable","reason":"The Hexaemeron passage is absent from this local snapshot; its bee and king wording cannot be checked.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-490832fc526f28db823d","ref":"Gospel of Thomas 107","status":"unavailable","reason":"The Thomas saying is absent from this local snapshot, so the proposed search for the lost sheep cannot be verified.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c0ea5fe48fe773c35e6e","ref":"Gospel of Thomas 107","status":"unavailable","reason":"The Thomas saying is absent from this local snapshot; its alleged preference for the one sheep is unverified.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-381569836f9f4eecf89e","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.9.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul's flock-milk analogy concerns a worker's material support; it does not add to the owner, lost animal or front guide scene in these paragraphs.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.9.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2ed83e407478115a0ac3","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.9.9","status":"rejected","reason":"The ox law is quoted here, but the human-worker application appears in 9:10 and addresses labor compensation, not the section's lost or leading herd.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.9.9","SBLGNT:1Cor.9.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-65391ce914f79e27ac51","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25","status":"rejected","reason":"Its wandering-sheep return is real, but Psalm 119:176 and Luke 15:4-7 already supply both prayer and recovery with closer animal imagery at paragraph 52.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-50a5c0222fffa6893c28","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25","status":"rejected","reason":"The return to the overseer is the same restoration point already represented by the Psalm 119 and Luke 15 annotations; no distinct leading-before movement occurs in this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0462890dbfe25a5d38ef","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The command assigns shepherding of God's flock to human overseers, distinguishing flock owner from delegated guide.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.20.28","SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2","SBLGNT:1Pet.5.3"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9e675cb933f386f1afc6","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse explicitly says God's flock is to be shepherded; 5:3 defines the delegated leader's manner.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.20.28","SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2","SBLGNT:1Pet.5.3"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f61629509553ed00614f","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.5.4","status":"rejected","reason":"The chief-shepherd title establishes rank over church elders; it does not depict the herd's front animal, pasture, or a lost animal.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.5.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-83e5c5b3c079544b4fb0","ref":"SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek explicitly joins abandoning a straight road with going astray, matching the paragraph 54 road turn.","paragraphs":[54],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15","SBLGNT:Acts.13.10"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c90d00877b2fddab77cf","ref":"SBLGNT:2Tim.2.19","status":"rejected","reason":"The seal belongs to God's foundation and carries a written declaration; the verse does not mark an animal or show an unidentified owner.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Tim.2.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1657b149a1d54b02fe9d","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.13.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The rebuke explicitly calls the Lord's paths straight and accuses someone of bending them, a precise road counterpart.","paragraphs":[54],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15","SBLGNT:Acts.13.10"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-89302769a55108496c84","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.20.28","status":"accepted","reason":"God's acquired flock is entrusted to appointed overseers, illuminating owner and guide as distinct roles.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.20.28","SBLGNT:1Pet.5.2","SBLGNT:1Pet.5.3"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-292503a0ca11f5168b4e","ref":"SBLGNT:Jas.3.3","status":"rejected","reason":"James's bit is placed in a horse's mouth and lets a human turn it; the section's hādī is the neck or a leading animal, a different steering relation.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Jas.3.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2f8416053e12e187eb40","ref":"SBLGNT:John.1.38","status":"rejected","reason":"The Greek transliterates Rabbi and glosses 'teacher'; this verse supplies no ownership or flock image and does not itself establish the proposed Semitic etymology.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.1.38"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-641275afd6fe413eab82","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.11","status":"rejected","reason":"The good shepherd's self-sacrifice is explicit but introduces a death motif absent from the owner-and-stray image; the following owner contrast is captured from 10:12-14.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-970be72532fb9733c056","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.12","status":"accepted","reason":"The hired hand's nonownership and flight explicitly contrast with the good shepherd's mutual recognition in 10:14.","paragraphs":[52],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.12","SBLGNT:John.10.14"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-cdaca278560bcc1b3059","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Verse 13 says the hired hand does not care, but nonownership and flight are stated in verse 12; the proposed ownership wording is assigned to the wrong verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.13","SBLGNT:John.10.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9e9375fc184dbe125fe9","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.14","status":"accepted","reason":"Good shepherd and sheep know one another, a positive counterpart to an animal whose owner is unknown.","paragraphs":[52],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.12","SBLGNT:John.10.14"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7d909ed8138d3ff8133f","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.16","status":"rejected","reason":"The 'other sheep' are already claimed by Jesus and are not described as wild or ownerless; equating them with the wild herds would misread this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d4f3a17db8cbbf4c832f","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.27","status":"rejected","reason":"Recognition and following recur in John 10:3-5, already annotated where the front-leading movement is stated more concretely.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.27"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f0ba43fbc0e63cbfdba6","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.3","status":"accepted","reason":"The shepherd calls his own sheep by name and leads them out; this is the recognized-belonging side of the front-led flock scene.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.3","SBLGNT:John.10.4","SBLGNT:John.10.5"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1b989e81e149c39a603c","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.3","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse explicitly joins the shepherd's own named sheep to his leading them out; paragraph 51 develops both elements.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.3","SBLGNT:John.10.4","SBLGNT:John.10.5"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-21a0ba97edf7b0be58b0","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.4","status":"accepted","reason":"John describes a leader walking ahead and sheep following his known voice, matching the section's foreleader movement.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.3","SBLGNT:John.10.4","SBLGNT:John.10.5"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7c7d9425296d2b97357a","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The explicit front-and-follow sequence is expressed by the shared John 10:3-5 annotation.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.3","SBLGNT:John.10.4","SBLGNT:John.10.5"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fdafb13389f46e9b47ad","ref":"SBLGNT:John.10.5","status":"accepted","reason":"Refusing an unfamiliar voice makes recognition a condition of flock following, explicitly included in the John 10:3-5 note.","paragraphs":[51],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.10.3","SBLGNT:John.10.4","SBLGNT:John.10.5"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9ff5418e17ed437c386e","ref":"SBLGNT:John.20.16","status":"rejected","reason":"The Greek gives Rabboni and glosses it as teacher; it does not establish a livestock owner's title or the claimed cognate history by itself.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.20.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d334df44b5ffaec5240d","ref":"SBLGNT:John.21.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Jesus' charge to Peter is genuine delegated care, but Acts 20:28 and 1 Peter 5:2-3 already make the same owner/delegate distinction beside paragraph 51.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.21.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c17bd2d7549425915c39","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.13.15","status":"rejected","reason":"The stall-and-watering example is a Sabbath argument about ordinary animal care; it contains no lost animal or foreleader and adds little to these paragraph images.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.13.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2e4f4b38984815dd160b","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","status":"accepted","reason":"A man who owns a flock seeks its one lost sheep until he finds it, a concrete recovery scene for paragraph 52.","paragraphs":[52],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","SBLGNT:Luke.15.5","SBLGNT:Luke.15.6","SBLGNT:Luke.15.7"],"annotations":["S001-INC-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c539424ecb934bc2f6a2","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The one lost sheep is explicitly sought by the person who has the hundred sheep; the annotation expresses this owner search.","paragraphs":[52],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","SBLGNT:Luke.15.5","SBLGNT:Luke.15.6","SBLGNT:Luke.15.7"],"annotations":["S001-INC-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f867c92be193a620b4c8","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.5","status":"accepted","reason":"After finding the lost sheep the man carries it on his shoulders, a stated step of the recovery sequence in the annotation.","paragraphs":[52],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","SBLGNT:Luke.15.5","SBLGNT:Luke.15.6","SBLGNT:Luke.15.7"],"annotations":["S001-INC-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8fd597ba7d5908882340","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.6","status":"accepted","reason":"The found sheep is celebrated at home; the annotation states the owner's joy as the end of the recovery scene.","paragraphs":[52],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","SBLGNT:Luke.15.5","SBLGNT:Luke.15.6","SBLGNT:Luke.15.7"],"annotations":["S001-INC-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-174bb42d1a531fce9d05","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.7","status":"accepted","reason":"Verse 7 interprets the recovered sheep as a sinner who repents, an interpretation expressly included in the note.","paragraphs":[52],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","SBLGNT:Luke.15.5","SBLGNT:Luke.15.6","SBLGNT:Luke.15.7"],"annotations":["S001-INC-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5cc9179a811be0fc3f15","ref":"SBLGNT:Mark.14.27","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse applies Zechariah's struck shepherd to disciples' falling away; paragraph 53's concern is the need for a guide, and Jeremiah 50:6 supplies a more exact failed-guide counter-scene.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Mark.14.27"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c7c7deed0793749f5865","ref":"SBLGNT:Mark.6.34","status":"accepted","reason":"The shepherdless crowd receives Jesus' teaching, making the absence of a guide into a need for instruction.","paragraphs":[53],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.9.36","SBLGNT:Mark.6.34"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-112631c4dd5817da6ad7","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.11.29","status":"rejected","reason":"The invitation to take a yoke and learn is metaphorical discipleship; it does not describe a camel's neck or its role as a herd guide.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.11.29"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-aa9ba980e0e84f2bec37","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.12.11","status":"rejected","reason":"A sheep fallen into a pit remains a known owner's sheep; the accident is not the dālla's straying or unknown-owner condition.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.12.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-29c54ae757271bbad405","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.18.12","status":"rejected","reason":"Matthew's search for the wandering one is genuine, but Luke 15:4-7 already supplies the same one-of-a-hundred search plus its return and interpretation.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4d640203e305c758c3b8","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.18.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The shepherd's joy repeats the recovery celebration already stated from Luke 15:6 in paragraph 52.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a274d4dfe2b7c65bfa28","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.18.14","status":"rejected","reason":"The Father's will that no little one perish explains Matthew's parable but introduces a new referent beyond this paragraph's concrete owner-and-camel definition.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.14"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7119a54676f152b21698","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.32","status":"rejected","reason":"The shepherd separates sheep and goats in a judgment simile; neither an owned herd following its foreleader nor a lost animal appears here. The throne is in 25:31.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.25.32","SBLGNT:Matt.25.31"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3a06d9f5313b92f9a92f","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.33","status":"rejected","reason":"The right-left sorting continues the judgment simile but adds no guide, pasture, owner recognition, or straying to the section's scene.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.25.33"],"annotations":[]}
*** End Patch"));

text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Update File: /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s001/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.session-20261005/sec10/verdicts.jsonl
@@
 {"connection_id":"BC-3a06d9f5313b92f9a92f","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.33","status":"rejected","reason":"The right-left sorting continues the judgment simile but adds no guide, pasture, owner recognition, or straying to the section's scene.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.25.33"],"annotations":[]}
