{"connection_id":"BC-e69f22fd6821ce8a7ea7","ref":"WLC:Jonah.4.11","status":"accepted","reason":"God's stated pity for Nineveh includes its many animals, exactly the mercy-to-animals addition made beside paragraph 54.","paragraphs":[54],"evidence":["WLC:Jonah.4.11"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3e5154d5f2e957ade0ed","ref":"WLC:Josh.24.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Hebrew ʿ-b-d denotes serving the Lord in Joshua's choice; the verse has no domestication or tarred camel, so it does not support that specialized lexical image.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.24.15"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-fb2b6f40db5dac4af433","ref":"WLC:Mic.2.12","status":"rejected","reason":"Israel's gathered remnant is likened to a flock, but no stable pasture or foreleader is stated in this verse; Psalm 23:1-3 gives the settled pasture scene.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Mic.2.12"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-f7c870968082e0e0c8ed","ref":"WLC:Mic.2.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The king and Lord go ahead of the gathered people, but 1 Samuel 8:20 already gives the m-l-k front-going comparison, and Numbers 27:17 adds the sheep image.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Mic.2.13"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2d666d7c817305d01591","ref":"WLC:Num.10.33","status":"rejected","reason":"The ark goes before Israel to find rest, but it is a sacred object and no herd or human guide is named; Numbers 27:17 fits the flock image directly.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Num.10.33"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-12e8db6d457c65cf3e92","ref":"WLC:Num.10.33","status":"rejected","reason":"Searching for a resting place after going ahead is explicit, but the ark's movement is not the leading animal or owner described in paragraph 51.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Num.10.33"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b8b53d3afdc61f0cdc79","ref":"WLC:Num.22.23","status":"accepted","reason":"The donkey sees the angel and turns off the road, giving paragraph 54 a genuine animal-better-than-rider counter-scene.","paragraphs":[54],"evidence":["WLC:Num.22.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-8528913a5187603a5d9e","ref":"WLC:Num.22.23","status":"rejected","reason":"The Balaam donkey story is a thematic reverse, not a Qur'anic counter-narrative of the same story; 1:7 does not retell Numbers 22.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Num.22.23"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-9ffc7072ee0d04d579c8","ref":"WLC:Num.27.17","status":"accepted","reason":"Moses requests a leader so the community will not be sheep without a shepherd, directly matching paragraph 53.","paragraphs":[53],"evidence":["WLC:Num.27.17"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-004"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-fe148d10cafafc3f7de5","ref":"WLC:Num.27.17","status":"accepted","reason":"The leader goes before and brings the people in, explicitly tied to avoiding a shepherdless flock in the same verse.","paragraphs":[53],"evidence":["WLC:Num.27.17"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-004"]}
